{code,core,types,ui}: detach long-running commands via lofi.job*

A blocking lofi.bash call holds the agent loop until the child
finishes, which wrecks interactive builds, long test watchers, and
anything else with a streaming tail. Introduce a background job
surface that mirrors the existing sandbox affordances:

- Bindings on lofi: jobSpawn, jobStatus, jobRead, jobWait, jobKill,
  jobNotify. Each takes camelCase args and returns camelCase fields
  (timeoutMs, exitCode, durationMs, logPath, totalBytes, notify,
  notifyIntervalMs, notifyChanged).
- All spawn args actually wire through: timeoutMs kills the child,
  notify=false silences it, notifyIntervalMs enables periodic ticks
  without a follow-up jobNotify call. Passing the interval implies
  notify=true.
- The transcript stays small: job output streams to a per-job log
  file under the session tmp dir, and only a bounded tail is shown
  to the model on completion.
- A new /job UI command surfaces live jobs and their exit status;
  periodic ticks land in the UI's notification slot, not in the
  model-turn transcript.
- Docs (lofi-code/src/docs/api.md) and the system prompt move to
  the new camelCase surface.

The Rust side keeps snake_case identifiers internally; only the
JS-visible wire names move to camelCase for consistency with the
surrounding sandbox (lofi.bash already takes timeoutMs, etc.).